meta {
  name: Give the caller a person row
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/persons
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  X-School-Id: {{schoolCode}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "cas_user_id": "{{callerCasId}}",
    "first_name": "Collection",
    "last_name": "Runner",
    "email": "collection-runner@dps-nagpur.example"
  }
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 201
  res.body.cas_user_id: eq {{callerCasId}}
}

script:pre-request {
  // The CAS identity of whoever is running the collection, read out of the
  // token rather than configured, so the two can never disagree. With no token
  // the API runs with a development subject whose id is all zeroes, which is
  // the same id scripts/e2e.sh hardcodes for the same purpose.
  const token = bru.getEnvVar("token");
  if (!token) {
    console.log("no token set: attributing to the development subject, all zeroes. " +
      "Against a CAS-guarded server this run will 401 long before that matters.");
    bru.setEnvVar("callerCasId", "00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000");
  } else {
    const payload = token.split(".")[1];
    if (!payload) {
      throw new Error("the token in this environment is not a JWT, so its subject cannot be read. " +
        "Every attributed write in folders 06, 07 and 10 will fail until this request succeeds.");
    }
    const claims = JSON.parse(
      Buffer.from(payload.replace(/-/g, "+").replace(/_/g, "/"), "base64").toString("utf8")
    );
    if (!claims.sub) {
      throw new Error("the token carries no sub claim, so there is no identity to attribute writes to. " +
        "Every attributed write in folders 06, 07 and 10 will fail until this request succeeds.");
    }
    bru.setEnvVar("callerCasId", claims.sub);
  }
}

script:post-response {
  bru.setEnvVar("callerPersonId", res.body.id);
}

docs {
  First, because everything that records who did something depends on it.

  Several handlers attribute a write to the caller: author_person_id on a
  student note, owner_person_id on a library item, confirmed_by on a placement,
  and created_by, assigned_by, verified_by and uploaded_by elsewhere. The value
  is read from the authenticated subject and refused in the body - send it
  yourself and you get a 400 saying so - and the subject is resolved to a person
  by matching the token's sub against cas_user_id. No such row, no attribution,
  and the strict ones answer:

    400 cannot record author_person_id: the caller has no person record at this school

  That is not a hypothetical. Until this request existed, folder 07's notes and
  every attributed write behind them had never once succeeded, in any run, by
  anyone - and folder 12's deletes for them were deleting nothing, which is why
  a red run never made it obvious. The optional ones are worse: they do not fail,
  they just record nobody.

  Why here rather than in the folder that first trips over it: this is the
  earliest folder that creates people and it runs before every consumer -
  folder 06 assigns teachers and students, folder 07 writes notes and documents,
  folder 10 uploads. Creating it at the top of any of those would be too late
  for the others.

  The id is taken from the token rather than configured, so the row cannot end
  up describing someone other than the caller. With no token, the development
  subject's all-zero id is used instead, which is what scripts/e2e.sh does.
  The env vars it leaves behind are callerCasId and callerPersonId.

  A second run against the same database fails here with 409: cas_user_id is
  unique, because one identity is one person. That is true of this whole
  collection, which expects a database it has not run against yet.
}
